---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { CATEGORIES, MIN_COMPLETENESS_FOR_RANK } from "../config/weights";
import { href } from "../lib/url";
---

<Layout title="Methodology">
  <span class="eyebrow">How the comparison is built</span>
  <h1 class="display">The <em>methodology</em></h1>

  <p>
    This tool combines five categories into a single fit score (0-100) per program. Every weight
    below lives in one config file in the source code
    (<code>src/config/weights.ts</code>), so it can be retuned without touching any scoring logic —
    and this page is generated from that same file, so it never drifts out of sync with what was
    actually computed. These weights aren't shown by default; the <a href={href("/#customize")}>comparison page</a> asks
    you to set your own priorities with live sliders before showing any results at all.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="marker">&#9656;</span> Disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    Michael Maffie, who built and maintains this tool, is an Assistant Professor at Cornell
    University's Nolan School of Hotel Administration, one of the 20 programs compared here. Every
    program's raw metrics and normalized category scores are computed by the identical public
    formula described on this page, with no manual overrides for any program, including Cornell.
    Those scores are fixed before any weighting is applied. The comparison page doesn't show a
    default weighting at all; nothing is ranked until a reader sets their own priorities with the
    sliders, so no single set of weights, ours or anyone else's, determines the result shown. Every
    input is individually cited on that program's profile page, and the full scoring pipeline is
    open source, so the computation behind any program's numbers, Cornell's included, can be
    independently checked.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Category</th>
        <th>Weight</th>
        <th>Metrics (weight within category)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        CATEGORIES.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{c.label}</td>
            <td>{Math.round(c.weight * 100)}%</td>
            <td>
              <ul>
                {c.metrics.map((m) => (
                  <li>
                    {m.path} — {Math.round(m.weight * 100)}%
                    {m.direction === "lowerIsBetter" ? " (lower is better)" : ""}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2><span class="marker">&#9656;</span> Why this split</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Career outcomes &amp; industry reputation (30%)</strong> is the largest single bucket,
    echoing how US News weights undergraduate outcomes most heavily in its own methodology: for a
    professional degree like hospitality administration, what happens to graduates is the most
    direct signal of program quality.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Academic research &amp; faculty quality (25%)</strong> mirrors US News's faculty-resources
    and peer-assessment buckets, and QS's academic-reputation and citations-per-faculty categories —
    but weighted lower than QS's combined ~60% research/reputation emphasis, since hospitality is a
    vocational, apprenticeship-driven field rather than a pure research discipline. The dominant metric
    in this category, <code>facultyPubCitationIndex</code>, is a named-faculty citation count: for each
    program, we compile the current, named faculty roster from the program's own official directory,
    match each person to an OpenAlex author record (by name and institution, excluding unmatched
    people from the sum rather than guessing), and total their career citations across every
    publication — any journal or field, not just hospitality-tagged ones, since a faculty member's
    broader research record is still evidence of their caliber. This replaced an earlier,
    institution-level version of the metric (any hospitality-tagged paper by anyone ever affiliated
    with the host university) that conflated host-university size with the specific program's faculty
    quality. A handful of programs' totals are still partial — pending a follow-up pass blocked by an
    external API's daily rate limit — and are flagged as such on their profile pages rather than
    treated as a completed result.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Academic quality &amp; completion (15%)</strong> is a newer addition, tracking whether
    students who enroll actually finish: six-year graduation rate, first-to-second-year retention, and
    faculty-student ratio. This is the single largest factor in US News's own undergraduate
    methodology (graduation and retention together typically run 17-20% there) — a program can have
    excellent outcomes for the students who make it to graduation while still losing a large share of
    its cohort along the way, which outcomes and admissions data alone wouldn't surface. For now this
    data comes from IPEDS/College Navigator and is university-wide rather than program-specific (the
    federal data doesn't break completion out by major) — flagged on every program's profile page.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Admissions selectivity (15%)</strong> is deliberately modest, echoing US News's own recent
    de-emphasis of test-score-driven selectivity in its undergraduate methodology.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Internship / placement pipeline (15%)</strong> has no direct analog in US News or QS. It is
    included because hospitality education is built around supervised work experience, and
    hospitality-specific rankings (e.g. CEOWORLD's) treat placement-pipeline strength as a
    first-order signal distinct from the outcomes it eventually produces.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="marker">&#9656;</span> Normalization</h2>
  <p>
    Every raw metric (dollars, percentages, ratios, counts) is converted to a 0-100 scale via
    min-max normalization <em>against the current set of ranked programs</em> — so scores are always
    relative to the active field, not fixed thresholds, and are recomputed every time the pipeline
    runs. Metrics where lower is better (like acceptance rate) are inverted before averaging.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a program is missing a metric, that metric is excluded and the remaining metrics in its
    category are re-weighted proportionally — a program is never penalized to a worst-possible score
    just because one data point hasn't been sourced yet. Instead, each program's profile page and the
    rankings table show a <strong>data completeness</strong> percentage, so readers can see how much
    of a program's score rests on verified data versus how much is still outstanding.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="marker">&#9656;</span> Minimum data to be ranked</h2>
  <p>
    A composite score computed from one or two sourced metrics isn't a meaningful comparison against
    a program with a dozen verified data points — but the re-weighting rule above means a sparse
    program's one strong metric can otherwise dominate its (nearly empty) composite score. To prevent
    data sparsity itself from producing a misleadingly high rank, a program needs at least {Math.round(MIN_COMPLETENESS_FOR_RANK * 100)}%
    of tracked metrics sourced to receive a numeric rank. Programs below that threshold are still
    scored and shown — nothing is hidden — but listed separately as <a href={href("/")}>not yet ranked</a> rather
    than competing against better-documented programs.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="marker">&#9656;</span> How this compares to other hospitality rankings</h2>
  <p>
    CEOWORLD Magazine publishes a well-known hospitality &amp; hotel management ranking, but its
    methodology is reputation/survey-weighted and does not disclose per-metric, per-program sourcing.
    This project's explicit goal is the opposite: every number behind every score is individually
    cited and dated, visible on each program's profile page and aggregated on the <a href={href("/sources/")}>sources page</a>. Where a reliable public source doesn't yet exist for a
    metric — recruiter reputation is the clearest example — that gap is stated outright rather than
    filled with an estimate.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.5vw, 3.2rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .marker {
    color: var(--accent);
    display: inline-block;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  thead th {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  code {
    background: var(--accent-bg);
    padding: 0.1rem 0.3rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }
</style>
